    {
      "ancestors": [],
      "attributes": [
        {
          "doc_text": " A wrapper for `&[u8]` representing a human-readable string that's conventionally, but not",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            299,
            392
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " always, UTF-8.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            393,
            411
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            412,
            415
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " Unlike `&str`, this type permits non-UTF-8 contents, making it suitable for user input,",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            416,
            507
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " non-native filenames (as `Path` only supports native filenames), and other applications that",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            508,
            604
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " need to round-trip whatever data the user provides.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            605,
            660
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            661,
            664
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " For an owned, growable byte string buffer, use",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            665,
            715
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " [`ByteString`](../../std/bstr/struct.ByteString.html).",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            716,
            774
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            775,
            778
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " `ByteStr` implements `Deref` to `[u8]`, so all methods available on `[u8]` are available on",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            779,
            874
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " `ByteStr`.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            875,
            889
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            890,
            893
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " # Representation",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            894,
            914
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            915,
            918
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " A `&ByteStr` has the same representation as a `&str`. That is, a `&ByteStr` is a wide pointer",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            919,
            1016
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " which includes a pointer to some bytes and a length.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1017,
            1073
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1074,
            1077
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " # Trait implementations",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1078,
            1105
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1106,
            1109
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " The `ByteStr` type has a number of trait implementations, and in particular, defines equality",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1110,
            1207
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " and comparisons between `&ByteStr`, `&str`, and `&[u8]`, for convenience.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1208,
            1285
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1286,
            1289
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " The `Debug` implementation for `ByteStr` shows its bytes as a normal string, with invalid UTF-8",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1290,
            1389
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " presented as hex escape sequences.",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1390,
            1428
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": "",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1429,
            1432
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " The `Display` implementation behaves as if the `ByteStr` were first lossily converted to a",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1433,
            1527
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": " `str`, with invalid UTF-8 presented as the Unicode replacement character (�).",
          "path": "doc",
          "range": [
            1528,
            1611
          ],
          "style": "outer"
        },
        {
          "doc_text": null,
          "path": "unstable",
          "range": [
